nit-I\r\n2008: \r\n2009: 99\r\n2010: \r\n2011: \f*\r\n2012: \r\n2013: Unit-I\r\n2014: \r\n2015: 100\r\n2016: \r\n2017: \f*\r\n2018: \r\n2019: Unit-I\r\n2020: \r\n2021: 101\r\n2022: \r\n2023: \f*\r\n2024: \r\n2025: Unit-I\r\n2026: \r\n2027: 102\r\n2028: \r\n2029: \f*\r\n2030: \r\n2031: Unit-I\r\n2032: \r\n2033: 103\r\n2034: \r\n2035: \f*\r\n2036: \r\n2037: Unit-I\r\n2038: \r\n2039: 104\r\n2040: \r\n2041: \f*\r\n2042: \r\n2043: Unit-I\r\n2044: \r\n2045: 105\r\n2046: \r\n2047: \f*\r\n2048: \r\n2049: Unit-I\r\n2050: \r\n2051: 106\r\n2052: \r\n2053: \f*\r\n2054: \r\n2055: Unit-I\r\n2056: \r\n2057: 107\r\n2058: \r\n2059: \f*\r\n2060: \r\n2061: Unit-I\r\n2062: \r\n2063: 108\r\n2064: \r\n2065: \fMinimal DFA’s\r\n2066: \r\n2067: Two states p and q of a DFA are called \r\n2068: \r\n2069: indistinguishable if \r\n2070: δ* (p, w) ∈ F implies δ* (q, w) ∈ F ,\r\n2071: \r\n2072: and\r\n2073: \r\n2074: δ* (p, w) ∉ F implies δ* (q, w) ∉ F ,\r\n2075: \r\n2076: for all w ∈ Σ*.\r\n2077: \r\n2078: If there exists some string w ∈ Σ* such that \r\n2079: \r\n2080:  δ* (p, w) ∈ F  \r\n2081: \r\n2082: and\r\n2083: \r\n2084:  δ* (q, w) ∉ F \r\n2085: \r\n2086: or vice versa, then the states p and q are said to be \r\n2087: \r\n2088: distinguishable by string w.\r\n2089: \r\n2090: *\r\n2091: \r\n2092: Unit-I\r\n2093: \r\n2094: 109\r\n2095: \r\n2096: \fThe “Mark” procedure\r\n2097: \r\n2098: This procedure marks all pairs of distinguishable \r\n2099: states.\r\n2100: \r\n2101: 1.  Remove all inaccessible states.  \r\n2102: \r\n2103: 2.  Consider all pairs of states (p, q).  If p ∈ F and q ∉ \r\n2104: F or vice versa, mark the pair (p, q) as \r\n2105: distinguishable.\r\n2106: \r\n2107: 3.  Repeat the following step until no previously \r\n2108: unmarked pairs are marked:\r\n2109: \r\n2110: For all pairs (p, q) and all a ∈ Σ, compute δ (p, a) \r\n2111: = pa and δ (q, a) = qa.  If the pair (pa, qa) is  marked as \r\n2112: distinguishable, mark (p, q) as distinguishable.\r\n2113: \r\n2114: *\r\n2115: \r\n2116: Unit-I\r\n2117: \r\n2118: 110\r\n2119: \r\n2120: \fThe “Reduce” procedure\r\n2121: \r\n2122: Given a DFA M = (Q, Σ, δ, q0, F), we construct a \r\n2123: reduced DFA M’ = (Q’, Σ, δ’, q0’, F’) as follows:\r\n2124: 1.  Use procedure Mark to find all pairs of \r\n2125: distinguishable states.  Then from this find the sets \r\n2126: of indistinguishable states by partitioning the state \r\n2127: set Q of the DFA into disjoint subsets {qi, qj, …, qk}, \r\n2128: {ql, qm, …, qn}, …, such that any q ∈ Q occurs in \r\n2129: exactly one of these subsets, that elements in each \r\n2130: subset are indistinguishable, and that any two \r\n2131: elements from different subsets are distinguishable.\r\n2132: \r\n2133: *\r\n2134: \r\n2135: Unit-I\r\n2136: \r\n2137: 111\r\n2138: \r\n2139: \fThe “Reduce” procedure, cont.\r\n2140: \r\n2141: 2.  For each set {qi, qj, …, qk} of such \r\n2142: indistinguishable states, create a state labeled ij…k \r\n2143: for M.\r\n2144: \r\n2145: 3.  For each transition rule of the form δ (qr, a) = qp, \r\n2146: find the sets to which qr and qp belong.  If qr ∈ {qi, \r\n2147: qj, …, qk} and qp ∈ {ql, qm, …, qn}, add to δ’ a rule δ’ \r\n2148: (ij…k, a) = lm…n.\r\n2149: \r\n2150: 4.  The initial state q0’ is that state of M’ whose label \r\n2151: includes the 0.\r\n2152: \r\n2153: 5.  F’ is the set of all the states whose label contains \r\n2154: i such that qi ∈ F.\r\n2155: \r\n2156: *\r\n2157: \r\n2158: Unit-I\r\n2159: \r\n2160: 112\r\n2161: \r\n2162: \fTheorem 2.4\r\n2163: \r\n2164: Given any DFA M, application of the procedure \r\n2165: Reduce yields another DFA M’ such that\r\n2166: \r\n2167: L(M) = L(M’)\r\n2168: \r\n2169: Furthermore, M’ is minimal in the sense that there is \r\n2170: no other DFA with a smaller number of states which \r\n2171: also accepts L(M).\r\n2172: \r\n2173: *\r\n2174: \r\n2175: Unit-I\r\n2176: \r\n2177: 113\r\n2178: \r\n2179: \fMinimal DFA’s\r\n2180: \r\n2181: Example:  This DFA can be reduced to the DFA on the next \r\n2182: \r\n2183: slide.\r\n2184: \r\n2185: *\r\n2186: \r\n2187: Unit-I\r\n2188: \r\n2189: 114\r\n2190: \r\n2191: \fMinimal DFA’s\r\n2192: \r\n2193: What are the distinguishable pairs?  \r\n2194: Mark step 2 gives (q0, q4), (q1, q4), (q2, q4) and (q3, q4).\r\n2195: Step 3 computes δ(q1, 1) = q4 and δ(q0, 1) = q3.  Since (q3, q4) \r\n2196: is a distinguishable pair, (q0, q1) is also marked as a \r\n2197: distinguishable pair.  Eventually the pairs (q0, q1), (q0, q2), \r\n2198: (q0, q3), (q0, q4), (q1, q4), (q2, q4) and (q3, q4) are marked as \r\n2199: distinguishable.  \r\n2200: \r\n2201: The remaining pairs, (q1, q2), (q1, q3), and (q2, q3) are \r\n2202: \r\n2203: undistinguishable.\r\n2204: \r\n2205: The states are partitioned into the sets {q0}, {q1, q2, q3}, and \r\n2206: \r\n2207: {q4}.\r\n2208: \r\n2209: *\r\n2210: \r\n2211: Unit-I\r\n2212: \r\n2213: 115\r\n2214: \r\n2215: \fMinimal DFA’s\r\n2216: \r\n2217: This is the reduced DFA resulting from the procedure.\r\n2218: \r\n2219: *\r\n2220: \r\n2221: Unit-I\r\n2222: \r\n2223: 116\r\n2224: \r\n2225: \fMinimum number of states in an FA\r\n2226: \r\n2227: If there are n distinguishable strings in a \r\n2228: \r\n2229: language, then there must be at least n \r\n2230: states in the finite automata that accepts it.\r\n2231: The FA has no memory, other than the current \r\n2232: \r\n2233: state.\r\n2234: \r\n2235: This puts a lower bound on the number of \r\n2236: states in a FA recognizing a language.\r\n2237: \r\n2238: *\r\n2239: \r\n2240: Unit-I\r\n2241: \r\n2242: 117\r\n2243: \r\n2244: \fFor\r\n2245: \r\n2246: ■ Equivalence of DFA and NFA, refer page \r\n2247: \r\n2248: no 33\r\n2249: \r\n2250: ■ FA’s with ε-transitions, refer page no. 43\r\n2251: ■ Equivalence and minimization of DFA, \r\n2252: \r\n2253: refer page no. 118 \r\n2254: \r\n2255: ■ All from Lecture Notes#01\r\n2256: \r\n2257: *\r\n2258: \r\n2259: Unit-I\r\n2260: \r\n2261: 118\r\n2262: \r\n2263: \f*\r\n2264: \r\n2265: Unit-I\r\n2266: \r\n2267: 1