{"step_index":42,"source":"MODEL","type":"GENERIC","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-09-05T16:18:53Z","content":"Created At: 2026-09-05T21:48:53+05:30\nCompleted At: 2026-09-05T21:48:54+05:30\nFile Path: `file:///C:/Users/tanus/.gemini/antigravity/brain/a2d53718-58c1-49c0-9cb0-9ac51d4c37cc/closure.txt`\nTotal Lines: 535\nTotal Bytes: 12179\nShowing lines 1 to 535\nThe following code has been modified to include a line number before every line, in the format: <line_number>: <original_line>. Please note that any changes targeting the original code should remove the line number, colon, and leading space.\n1: 1 Closure Properties\r\n2: \r\n3: Closure Properties\r\n4: \r\n5: • Recall that we can carry out operations on one or more languages to obtain a new language\r\n6: \r\n7: • Very useful in studying the properties of one language by relating it to other (better under-\r\n8: \r\n9: stood) languages\r\n10: \r\n11: • Most useful when the operations are sophisticated, yet are guaranteed to preserve interesting\r\n12: \r\n13: properties of the language.\r\n14: \r\n15: • Today: A variety of operations which preserve regularity\r\n16: \r\n17: – i.e., the universe of regular languages is closed under these operations\r\n18: \r\n19: Closure Properties\r\n20: \r\n21: Deﬁnition 1. Regular Languages are closed under an operation op on languages if\r\n22: \r\n23: L1, L2, . . . Ln regular =⇒ L = op(L1, L2, . . . Ln) is regular\r\n24: \r\n25: Example 2. Regular languages are closed under\r\n26: \r\n27: • “halving”, i.e., L regular =⇒ 1\r\n28: \r\n29: 2 L regular.\r\n30: \r\n31: • “reversing”, i.e., L regular =⇒ Lrev regular.\r\n32: \r\n33: 1.1 Boolean Operators\r\n34: \r\n35: Operations from Regular Expressions\r\n36: \r\n37: Proposition 3. Regular Languages are closed under ∪, ◦ and ∗.\r\n38: \r\n39: Proof. (Summarizing previous arguments.)\r\n40: \r\n41: • L1, L2 regular =⇒ ∃ regexes R1, R2 s.t. L1 = L(R1) and L2 = L(R2).\r\n42: \r\n43: – =⇒ L1 ∪ L2 = L(R1 ∪ R2) =⇒ L1 ∪ L2 regular.\r\n44: – =⇒ L1 ◦ L2 = L(R1 ◦ R2) =⇒ L1 ◦ L2 regular.\r\n45: – =⇒ L∗\r\n46: \r\n47: 1) =⇒ L∗\r\n48: \r\n49: 1 = L(R∗\r\n50: \r\n51: 1 regular.\r\n52: \r\n53: Closure Under Complementation\r\n54: \r\n55: 2\r\n56: \r\n57: \fProposition 4. Regular Languages are closed under complementation, i.e., if L is regular then\r\n58: L = Σ∗ \\ L is also regular.\r\n59: \r\n60: Proof.\r\n61: \r\n62: • If L is regular, then there is a DFA M = (Q, Σ, δ, q0, F ) such that L = L(M ).\r\n63: \r\n64: • Then, M = (Q, Σ, δ, q0, Q \\ F ) (i.e., switch accept and non-accept states) accepts L.\r\n65: \r\n66: What happens if M (above) was an NFA?\r\n67: \r\n68: Closure under ∩\r\n69: \r\n70: Proposition 5. Regular Languages are closed under intersection, i.e., if L1 and L2 are regular\r\n71: then L1 ∩ L2 is also regular.\r\n72: \r\n73: Proof. Observe that L1 ∩ L2 = L1 ∪ L2. Since regular languages are closed under union and\r\n74: complementation, we have\r\n75: \r\n76: • L1 and L2 are regular\r\n77: \r\n78: • L1 ∪ L2 is regular\r\n79: \r\n80: • Hence, L1 ∩ L2 = L1 ∪ L2 is regular.\r\n81: \r\n82: Is there a direct proof for intersection (yielding a smaller DFA)?\r\n83: \r\n84: Cross-Product Construction\r\n85: Let M1 = (Q1, Σ, δ1, q1, F1) and M2 = (Q2, Σ, δ2, q2, F2) be DFAs recognizing L1 and L2, respec-\r\n86: tively.\r\n87: \r\n88: Idea: Run M1 and M2 in parallel on the same input and accept if both M1 and M2 accept.\r\n89: \r\n90: Consider M = (Q, Σ, δ, q0, F ) deﬁned as follows\r\n91: \r\n92: • Q = Q1 × Q2\r\n93: \r\n94: • q0 = (cid:104)q1, q2(cid:105)\r\n95: \r\n96: • δ((cid:104)p1, p2(cid:105), a) = (cid:104)δ1(p1, a), δ2(p2, a)(cid:105)\r\n97: \r\n98: • F = F1 × F2\r\n99: \r\n100: M accepts L1 ∩ L2 (exercise)\r\n101: What happens if M1 and M2 where NFAs? Still works! Set δ((cid:104)p1, p2(cid:105), a) = δ1(p1, a) × δ2(p2, a).\r\n102: \r\n103: An Example\r\n104: \r\n105: 3\r\n106: \r\n107: \f1\r\n108: \r\n109: q0\r\n110: 0\r\n111: \r\n112: 0\r\n113: \r\n114: 0\r\n115: \r\n116: ×\r\