e homomorphism as an operation on regular expressions\r\n203: \r\n204: • Show that L(h(R)) = h(L(R))\r\n205: \r\n206: • Let R be such that L = L(R). Let R(cid:48) = h(R). Then h(L) = L(R(cid:48)).\r\n207: \r\n208: Homomorphism as an Operation on Regular Expressions\r\n209: \r\n210: Deﬁnition 11. For a regular expression R, let h(R) be the regular expression obtained by replacing\r\n211: each occurence of a ∈ Σ in R by the string h(a).\r\n212: \r\n213: Example 12. If R = (0∪1)∗001(0∪1)∗ and h(0) = ab and h(1) = bc then h(R) = (ab∪bc)∗ababbc(ab∪\r\n214: bc)∗\r\n215: \r\n216: Formally h(R) is deﬁned inductively as follows.\r\n217: \r\n218: h(∅) = ∅\r\n219: h((cid:15)) = (cid:15)\r\n220: h(a) = h(a) h(R∗) = (h(R))∗\r\n221: \r\n222: h(R1R2) = h(R1)h(R2)\r\n223: h(R1 ∪ R2) = h(R2) ∪ h(R2)\r\n224: \r\n225: Proof of Claim\r\n226: \r\n227: Claim\r\n228: For any regular expression R, L(h(R)) = h(L(R)).\r\n229: \r\n230: Proof. By induction on the number of operations in R\r\n231: \r\n232: • Base Cases: For R = (cid:15) or ∅, h(R) = R and h(L(R)) = L(R). For R = a, L(R) = {a} and\r\n233: \r\n234: h(L(R)) = {h(a)} = L(h(a)) = L(h(R)). So claim holds.\r\n235: \r\n236: • Induction Step: For R = R1 ∪ R2, observe that h(R) = h(R1) ∪ h(R2) and h(L(R)) =\r\n237: h(L(R1) ∪ L(R2)) = h(L(R1)) ∪ h(L(R2)). By induction hypothesis, h(L(Ri)) = L(h(Ri))\r\n238: and so h(L(R)) = L(h(R1) ∪ h(R2))\r\n239: Other cases (R = R1R2 and R = R∗\r\n240: \r\n241: 1) similar.\r\n242: \r\n243: Nonregularity and Homomorphism\r\n244: \r\n245: If L is not regular, is h(L) also not regular?\r\n246: \r\n247: • No! Consider L = {0n1n | n ≥ 0} and h(0) = a and h(1) = (cid:15). Then h(L) = a∗.\r\n248: \r\n249: Applying a homomorphism can “simplify” a non-regular language into a regular language.\r\n250: \r\n251: 5\r\n252: \r\n253: \f1.3 Inverse Homomorphism\r\n254: \r\n255: Inverse Homomorphism\r\n256: \r\n257: Deﬁnition 13. Given homomorphism h : Σ∗ → ∆∗ and L ⊆ ∆∗, h−1(L) = {w ∈ Σ∗ | h(w) ∈ L}\r\n258: \r\n259: h−1(L) consists of strings whose homomorphic images are in L\r\n260: \r\n261: h\r\n262: \r\n263: Σ∗\r\n264: \r\n265: h−1(L)\r\n266: \r\n267: ∆∗\r\n268: \r\n269: L\r\n270: \r\n271: Inverse Homomorphism\r\n272: \r\n273: Example 14. Let Σ = {a, b}, and ∆ = {0, 1}. Let L = (00 ∪ 1)∗ and h(a) = 01 and h(b) = 10.\r\n274: \r\n275: • h−1(1001) = {ba}, h−1(010110) = {aab}\r\n276: \r\n277: • h−1(L) = (ba)∗\r\n278: \r\n279: • What is h(h−1(L))? (1001)∗ (cid:40) L\r\n280: \r\n281: Note: In general h(h−1(L)) ⊆ L ⊆ h−1(h(L)), but neither containment is necessarily an equal-\r\n282: \r\n283: ity.\r\n284: Closure under Inverse Homomorphism\r\n285: \r\n286: Proposition 15. Regular languages are closed under inverse homomorphism, i.e., if L is regular\r\n287: and h is a homomorphism then h−1(L) is regular.\r\n288: \r\n289: Proof. We will use the representation of regular languages in terms of DFA to argue this.\r\n290: \r\n291: Given a DFA M recognizing L, construct an DFA M (cid:48) that accepts h−1(L)\r\n292: \r\n293: • Intuition: On input w M (cid:48) will run M on h(w) and accept if M does.\r\n294: \r\n295: Closure under Inverse Homomorphism\r\n296: \r\n297: 6\r\n298: \r\n299: \f• Intuition: On input w M (cid:48) will run M on h(w) and accept if M does.\r\n300: \r\n301: Example 16. L = L ((00 ∪ 1)∗). h(a) = 01, h(b) = 10.\r\n302: \r\n303: 0, 1\r\n304: \r\n305: q2\r\n306: \r\n307: 0\r\n308: \r\n309: 0\r\n310: \r\n311: 1\r\n312: \r\n313: q1\r\n314: \r\n315: 0, 1\r\n316: \r\n317: a, b\r\n318: \r\n319: q2\r\n320: \r\n321: b\r\n322: 0\r\n323: \r\n324: 0\r\n325: a\r\n326: \r\n327: b\r\n328: \r\n329: 1\r\n330: \r\n331: q1\r\n332: \r\n333: 1\r\n334: \r\n335: q0\r\n336: \r\n337: 1\r\n338: \r\n339: a\r\n340: \r\n341: q0\r\n342: \r\n343: Closure under Inverse Homomorphism\r\n344: Formal Construction\r\n345: \r\n346: • Let M = (Q, ∆, δ, q0, F ) accept L ⊆ ∆∗ and let h : Σ∗ → ∆∗ be a homomorphism\r\n347: \r\n348: • Deﬁne M (cid:48) = (Q(cid:48), Σ, δ(cid:48), q(cid:48)\r\n349: \r\n350: 0, F (c