ular?\r\n413: \r\n414: Note that M does not necessarily accept all strings in L; L ⊆ L(M ).\r\n415: By applying a series of regularity preserving operations to L(M ) we will construct L, thus\r\n416: \r\n417: showing that L is regular\r\n418: \r\n419: Computations: Valid and Invalid\r\n420: \r\n421: 8\r\n422: \r\n423: \f• Consider an alphabet ∆ consisting of [paq] where p, q ∈ Q, a ∈ Σ and δ(p, a) = q. So symbols\r\n424: \r\n425: of ∆ represent transitions of M .\r\n426: \r\n427: • Let h : ∆ → Σ∗ be a homomorphism such that h([paq]) = a\r\n428: \r\n429: • L1 = h−1(L(M )); L1 contains strings of L(M ) where each symbol is associated with a pair\r\n430: \r\n431: of states that represent some transition\r\n432: \r\n433: – Some strings of L1 represent valid computations of M . But there are also other strings\r\n434: \r\n435: in L1 which do not correspond to valid computations of M\r\n436: \r\n437: • We will ﬁrst remove all the strings from L1 that correspond to invalid computations, and\r\n438: \r\n439: then remove those that do not visit every state at least once.\r\n440: \r\n441: Only Valid Computations\r\n442: Strings of ∆∗ that represent valid computations of M satisfy the following conditions\r\n443: \r\n444: • The ﬁrst state in the ﬁrst symbol must be q0\r\n445: \r\n446: L2 = L1 ∩ (([q0a1q1] ∪ [q0a2q2] ∪ · · · ∪ [q0akqk])∆∗)\r\n447: \r\n448: ([q0a1q1], . . . [q0akqk] are all the transitions out of q0 in M )\r\n449: \r\n450: • The ﬁrst state in one symbol must equal the second state in previous symbol\r\n451: \r\n452: L3 = L2 \\ (∆∗(\r\n453: \r\n454: (cid:88)\r\n455: \r\n456: [paq][rbs])∆∗)\r\n457: \r\n458: q(cid:54)=r\r\n459: \r\n460: Remove “invalid” sequences from L2. Diﬀerence of two regular languages is regular ( why?).\r\n461: So L3 is regular.\r\n462: \r\n463: • The second state of the last symbol must be in F . Holds trivially because L3 only contains\r\n464: \r\n465: strings accepted by M\r\n466: \r\n467: Example continued\r\n468: \r\n469: So far, regular language L3 = set of strings in ∆∗ that represent valid computations of M .\r\n470: \r\n471: • Let Eq ⊆ ∆ be the set of symbols where q appears neither as the ﬁrst nor the second state.\r\n472: \r\n473: Then E∗\r\n474: \r\n475: q is the set of strings where q never occurs.\r\n476: \r\n477: • We remove from L3 those strings where some q ∈ Q never occurs\r\n478: \r\n479: L4 = L3 \\ (\r\n480: \r\n481: E∗\r\n482: q )\r\n483: \r\n484: (cid:91)\r\n485: \r\n486: q∈Q\r\n487: \r\n488: • Finally we discard the state components in L4\r\n489: \r\n490: • Hence, L is regular.\r\n491: \r\n492: L = h(L4)\r\n493: \r\n494: 9\r\n495: \r\n496: \f2.3 In a nutshell . . .\r\n497: \r\n498: Proving Regularity and Non-Regularity\r\n499: Showing that L is not regular\r\n500: \r\n501: • Use the pumping lemma\r\n502: \r\n503: • Or, show that from L you can obtain a known non-regular language through regularity pre-\r\n504: \r\n505: serving operations.\r\n506: \r\n507: • Note: Non-regular languages are not closed under the operations discussed.\r\n508: \r\n509: Showing that L is regular\r\n510: \r\n511: • Construct a DFA or NFA or regular expression recognizing L\r\n512: \r\n513: • Or, show that L can be obtained from known regular languages L1, L2, . . . Lk through regu-\r\n514: \r\n515: larity preserving operations\r\n516: \r\n517: • Note: Do not use pumping lemma to prove regularity!!\r\n518: \r\n519: A list of Regularity-Preserving Operations\r\n520: \r\n521: Regular languages are closed under the following operations.\r\n522: \r\n523: • Regular Expression operations\r\n524: \r\n525: • Boolean operations: union, intersection, complement\r\n526: \r\n527: • Homomorphism\r\n528: \r\n529: • Inverse Homomorphism\r\n530: \r\n531: (And several other operations...)\r\n532: \r\n533: 10\r\n534: \r\n535: \f\nThe above content shows the entire, complete file contents of the requested file.\n"}
