ince both are accepting and their transitions on 0 and 1 mirror each other consistently.\n\nChecking further, every combination keeps landing on accepting states — S4 on 00 and S5 on 00 both reach S4, and other pairs like 01 both stay accepting too. It's starting to look like all four states S4, S5, S6, S7 might actually be equivalent, since I can't find any path that diverges into a non-accepting state.\n\nFinishing the ACC state loops back to itself on both inputs, since it's fully accepting territory now. Comparing S2 and S3 shows they're distinguishable since S2 reaches an accepting state in one step on 0 while S3 doesn't, and the same logic separates S1 from S2. Now I'm checking whether S1 and S3 are also distinguishable by tracing their transitions on 0.\n\nThe four states end up being distinct, so the minimized DFA keeps all of S1, S2, S3, and ACC, giving a transition table where S1 goes to S2 and S3 on 0 and 1, S2 goes to ACC and S3, S3 goes to S2 and ACC, and ACC loops to itself. This structure recognizes strings containing \"00\" or \"11\" — which lines up nicely with the earlier ε-NFA description. Now I'm moving on to Q6, the Mealy Machine question.\n\nFor part a, I'm designing a machine that tracks the last one or two symbols seen, since the output depends on whether the input ends in \"10\" or \"11\". I'm working out that I need states representing \"no relevant history,\" \"last symbol was 1,\" and possibly \"last two symbols were 10,\" then figuring out how the Mealy outputs (A, B, or C) get emitted on each transition based on this tracked history.\n\nOn reading a 1, we transition to q₁, outputting C since a single character can never complete a \"10\" or \"11\" pattern by itself. So each step's output depends on whether the two-character window ending at that point matches, meaning I need to track the previous character as state and only emit A when the current pair matches \"10\" or \"11\".\n\nNow I'm moving on to converting a given Mealy machine in